1
00:00:03,170 --> 00:00:05,171
[metal rattling]

2
00:00:05,172 --> 00:00:06,924
[worker straining, groaning]

3
00:00:19,895 --> 00:00:23,022
Buddy, I'm gonna have to charge you
double for that.

4
00:00:23,023 --> 00:00:26,693
And if it turns out you're carrying tools,
it's triple...

5
00:00:30,864 --> 00:00:35,452
That should more than cover it.
Appreciate the ride, Ossie.

6
00:00:56,431 --> 00:01:02,437
Chad, Jo. You're both looking well.

7
00:01:05,482 --> 00:01:07,692
I'm guessing she's expecting me.

8
00:01:07,693 --> 00:01:09,820
[breathing heavily]

9
00:01:22,165 --> 00:01:23,166
Bernard.

10
00:01:23,917 --> 00:01:24,918
Camille.

11
00:01:29,756 --> 00:01:30,966
What do you want?

12
00:01:46,023 --> 00:01:49,234
[The Algorithm] <i>Bernard Holland.
Why are you here?</i>

13
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:54,406
I know drugs are in the water,
and that you have Juliette.

14
00:01:56,325 --> 00:01:58,535
I'm here to ask for her release.

15
00:01:59,411 --> 00:02:02,998
And for you to turn off the vitamin D+.

16
00:02:03,582 --> 00:02:04,791
And why would I do that?

17
00:02:06,043 --> 00:02:11,256
Because in return I'm giving you the gift
of narrative, which you need right now.

18
00:02:12,007 --> 00:02:16,427
Word is spreading out of Mechanical
that you brainwashed Juliette.

19
00:02:16,428 --> 00:02:18,388
I can save you from a rebellion.

20
00:02:20,265 --> 00:02:21,266
Is that true?

21
00:02:21,975 --> 00:02:25,687
[The Algorithm] <i>Social harmony
remains well below Mayor Jahns's tenure.</i>

22
00:02:26,271 --> 00:02:28,857
<i>Even below Mr. Holland's brief term.</i>

23
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:32,694
People are easily manipulated.

24
00:02:33,362 --> 00:02:37,616
All they need is someone worse,
and I will be that person for you.

25
00:02:38,951 --> 00:02:42,287
You tell them that I killed Meadows.

26
00:02:42,913 --> 00:02:47,500
You can also say that I ordered
the deaths of Jahns and Marnes,

27
00:02:47,501 --> 00:02:50,504
and that I lied
about Juliette wanting to go out.

28
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:55,217
Blame the unrest
of the past few months on me.

29
00:02:55,843 --> 00:02:57,177
None of it would be a lie.

30
00:02:57,761 --> 00:03:01,098
I was following orders, just like you are.

31
00:03:06,979 --> 00:03:08,397
And what happens after that?

32
00:03:08,897 --> 00:03:12,859
Rehabilitate me to show
what a merciful leader you are...

33
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:14,820
far more than I ever was.

34
00:03:16,154 --> 00:03:19,199
Then I will live my days out quietly.

35
00:03:21,410 --> 00:03:24,204
Go ahead. Run the numbers.

36
00:03:27,207 --> 00:03:29,793
[The Algorithm]
<i>The difference in outcomes is negligible.</i>

37
00:03:30,377 --> 00:03:31,961
<i>On the other hand,</i>

38
00:03:31,962 --> 00:03:34,714
<i>if we present the narrative
you've suggested,</i>

39
00:03:34,715 --> 00:03:37,134
<i>then send you outside to clean,</i>

40
00:03:37,718 --> 00:03:42,096
<i>and maintain the original plan
with Juliette Nichols and the vitamin D+,</i>

41
00:03:42,097 --> 00:03:44,474
<i>the Silo actually benefits.</i>

42
00:03:45,809 --> 00:03:47,227
That can't be right.

43
00:03:47,728 --> 00:03:48,979
You're missing something.

44
00:03:49,688 --> 00:03:52,064
People want redemption stories.

45
00:03:52,065 --> 00:03:56,611
Redemption is the commonality in all
the great myths, the major religions.

46
00:03:56,612 --> 00:03:58,571
[The Algorithm]
<i>Not only have you miscalculated,</i>

47
00:03:58,572 --> 00:04:02,451
<i>you have also failed to realize
that you have no leverage.</i>

48
00:04:03,368 --> 00:04:05,161
<i>You are already in custody,</i>

49
00:04:05,162 --> 00:04:09,332
<i>and you remain deeply unpopular
with 89% of the population.</i>

50
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:12,376
You're gonna take everything from me
after I've given my life to you?

51
00:04:12,377 --> 00:04:15,464
- [The Algorithm] <i>Goodbye, Bernard.</i>
- You're not an omniscient, are you?

52
00:04:16,048 --> 00:04:17,881
If you were actually omniscient,

53
00:04:17,882 --> 00:04:20,176
you wouldn't have led us here
to begin with.

54
00:04:20,177 --> 00:04:24,389
You wouldn't need to erase
everyone's memories or kill so many.

55
00:04:25,098 --> 00:04:27,517
You don't know
much more than I do, do you?

56
00:04:27,518 --> 00:04:28,894
[The Algorithm] <i>Goodbye, Bernard.</i>

57
00:04:41,156 --> 00:04:43,200
Did that turn out the way you expected?

58
00:04:44,826 --> 00:04:45,953
Yes.

59
00:04:46,912 --> 00:04:50,248
It's been the same voice
for the entire time I served.

60
00:04:50,249 --> 00:04:54,169
But I've never heard it end
a conversation like that. Have you?

61
00:04:55,212 --> 00:04:59,132
- No, but I also don't provoke it.
- Exactly.

62
00:04:59,883 --> 00:05:05,472
It wanted to stop talking
because of annoyance, frustration, anger.

63
00:05:06,098 --> 00:05:09,935
Rather unmachine-like characteristics,
wouldn't you say?

64
00:05:14,982 --> 00:05:20,362
One time, not long ago... it was strange.

65
00:05:21,405 --> 00:05:22,948
It consoled me.

66
00:05:24,241 --> 00:05:26,577
And you wondered whether it was human.

67
00:05:27,202 --> 00:05:28,786
How would that be possible?

68
00:05:28,787 --> 00:05:32,164
Someone whose voice
hasn't changed all these years

69
00:05:32,165 --> 00:05:34,960
and is always available to talk.

70
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:37,629
Same voice.

71
00:05:38,797 --> 00:05:40,716
Perhaps not the same person.

72
00:05:42,134 --> 00:05:44,136
I know one thing about a person.

73
00:05:46,847 --> 00:05:48,557
A person can be defeated.

74
00:07:49,052 --> 00:07:50,971
[Camille] <i>Good morning, citizens.</i>

75
00:07:51,555 --> 00:07:52,973
<i>I have important news.</i>

76
00:07:53,807 --> 00:07:59,228
<i>Former interim mayor and head of IT,
Bernard Holland, has been found alive.</i>

77
00:07:59,229 --> 00:08:00,688
[citizens murmuring, clamoring]

78
00:08:00,689 --> 00:08:02,565
For all his crimes against the Silo,

79
00:08:02,566 --> 00:08:05,444
he has been sentenced
by the judge to clean at noon today.

80
00:08:06,111 --> 00:08:08,822
<i>Please make plans to attend the viewing
in the nearest cafeteria.</i>

81
00:08:12,492 --> 00:08:15,036
<i>As always,
be courteous and safe on the stairs.</i>

82
00:08:15,037 --> 00:08:16,330
Thank you.

83
00:08:27,299 --> 00:08:28,342
[Hank] Sheriff!

84
00:08:29,134 --> 00:08:30,260
[sighs]

85
00:08:31,136 --> 00:08:32,595
- Did you hear that?
- Yeah.

86
00:08:32,596 --> 00:08:33,763
Then you know I can't stay.

87
00:08:33,764 --> 00:08:35,223
I just need 5 minutes.

88
00:08:35,224 --> 00:08:36,891
[on radio]
<i>Deputy Jerry for Sheriff Billings.</i>

89
00:08:36,892 --> 00:08:38,809
Don't answer that. Not yet, please.

90
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:42,188
I had that door reinforced.

91
00:08:43,148 --> 00:08:46,276
So whatever you're doing, just...
you should save your energy.

92
00:08:48,362 --> 00:08:53,574
But if we hit the joints at the same time,
maybe we can weaken the hinges, right?

93
00:08:53,575 --> 00:08:57,495
Even if you got out,
that hallway is full of raiders.

94
00:08:57,496 --> 00:08:59,872
Okay, okay. So, that's it?

95
00:08:59,873 --> 00:09:02,042
Well, the only way it ends differently

96
00:09:02,835 --> 00:09:05,879
is if my wife stops taking orders
from that fucking screen.

97
00:09:09,591 --> 00:09:12,719
I don't even know who she is right now.

98
00:09:14,388 --> 00:09:15,430
[sighs]

99
00:09:19,142 --> 00:09:21,644
[Martha] So, there's so much
we have to tell you,

100
00:09:21,645 --> 00:09:24,272
and we will,
but since your time is limited,

101
00:09:24,273 --> 00:09:26,775
I'm gonna start
with where we are this morning.

102
00:09:28,735 --> 00:09:33,490
[sighs] Bernard Holland offered himself up
to clean as a distraction.

103
00:09:36,326 --> 00:09:37,326
For what?

104
00:09:37,327 --> 00:09:38,953
I'll come to that.

105
00:09:38,954 --> 00:09:42,623
Knox tells me
we can only do what we need to do

106
00:09:42,624 --> 00:09:46,170
if we use something that you discovered.

107
00:09:48,213 --> 00:09:49,715
- You told her?
- No.

108
00:09:50,507 --> 00:09:52,300
Even my own wife wouldn't tell me.

109
00:09:52,301 --> 00:09:53,385
Not my call.

110
00:09:53,886 --> 00:09:55,095
And I didn't tell Shirl.

111
00:09:56,096 --> 00:09:57,598
Because... [scoffs]

112
00:09:58,932 --> 00:10:01,351
...this is such a big violation of the Pact.

113
00:10:02,102 --> 00:10:05,105
You have to be the one to show them.

114
00:10:11,653 --> 00:10:13,113
What is it you want me to do?

115
00:10:14,031 --> 00:10:18,118
Well, to start, we have to get
into water filtration on 55.

116
00:10:34,801 --> 00:10:36,803
[door opens]

117
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:40,515
Nichols, up.

118
00:10:40,516 --> 00:10:42,434
- She's still mayor, Chad.
- Get up.

119
00:10:43,060 --> 00:10:44,269
It's okay. I'm going.

120
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:47,689
[whispers] It's okay.

121
00:10:53,987 --> 00:10:55,989
[Chad] Hold it there. Hands.

122
00:10:59,368 --> 00:11:00,369
[door closes]

123
00:11:07,292 --> 00:11:10,587
[Knox, Paul strain, grunt]

124
00:11:17,261 --> 00:11:19,346
Go ahead. We've already seen it.

125
00:11:35,070 --> 00:11:36,363
[Teddy] What the fuck?

126
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:48,792
I heard you weren't much of a drinker,

127
00:11:49,376 --> 00:11:51,502
so I had these portered down
from your office.

128
00:11:51,503 --> 00:11:52,796
I didn't think you'd mind.

129
00:11:54,047 --> 00:11:55,132
Sure.

130
00:11:55,966 --> 00:11:57,176
Do you--

131
00:11:59,678 --> 00:12:00,846
Some water then?

132
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:04,933
So why am I here?

133
00:12:08,645 --> 00:12:09,646
Sit.

134
00:12:17,070 --> 00:12:18,071
[inhales sharply]

135
00:12:19,364 --> 00:12:22,075
[exhales deeply]

136
00:12:24,203 --> 00:12:27,706
Did your friends tell you
what happened during the rebellion?

137
00:12:29,041 --> 00:12:30,250
I got the highlights.

138
00:12:30,751 --> 00:12:33,253
Did they tell you Bernard tried
to starve them out?

139
00:12:34,254 --> 00:12:36,714
Stopped all food deliveries
to the Down Deep?

140
00:12:36,715 --> 00:12:39,593
Even had one of the kitchen workers
poison the pantry.

141
00:12:40,219 --> 00:12:43,847
I helped the rebels move the barricade up
ten floors so they'd have a farm.

142
00:12:47,142 --> 00:12:48,684
Your friends would have died,

143
00:12:48,685 --> 00:12:51,979
and their rebellion would have
been over if it wasn't for me.

144
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:53,814
And then they only won

145
00:12:53,815 --> 00:12:57,235
because Robert and I got the support
of the sheriff's deputies.

146
00:12:57,236 --> 00:13:00,155
Well, I'm sure you had your reasons.

147
00:13:02,491 --> 00:13:04,493
Bernard couldn't be trusted anymore.

148
00:13:05,118 --> 00:13:09,414
So I went against my orders,
against the Pact, to do what was right.

149
00:13:12,668 --> 00:13:13,877
[chuckles]

150
00:13:15,504 --> 00:13:18,966
[smacks lips] I know Bernard didn't die.

151
00:13:20,509 --> 00:13:22,636
In fact, we have him now.

152
00:13:23,804 --> 00:13:26,390
He's going out to clean at noon today.
You will preside as mayor.

153
00:13:28,559 --> 00:13:30,561
Oh, he turned himself in.

154
00:13:31,228 --> 00:13:33,564
And I presume he was
hiding down with your friends?

155
00:13:34,064 --> 00:13:36,148
Is that why I'm here?
You want me to confirm that?

156
00:13:36,149 --> 00:13:37,442
No, not at all.

157
00:13:38,861 --> 00:13:45,075
No, I-I asked you here
because I want us to work together.

158
00:13:48,745 --> 00:13:53,292
[chuckles] I think, um,
I'll take one of those now.

159
00:13:55,460 --> 00:13:56,461
[chuckles]

160
00:14:00,299 --> 00:14:04,594
Look, what's happening with the water
is an extreme measure,

161
00:14:04,595 --> 00:14:07,221
but I don't have
any other choice at this point.

162
00:14:07,222 --> 00:14:08,307
People know too much.

163
00:14:11,393 --> 00:14:12,394
Look,

164
00:14:12,978 --> 00:14:16,272
they're starting to ask questions,
in large part because of you.

165
00:14:16,273 --> 00:14:19,610
Questions that can,
and will, get us killed.

166
00:14:26,158 --> 00:14:28,951
[sniffles] Fucking hell. People like that?

167
00:14:28,952 --> 00:14:31,538
After half a glass, you don't really care.

168
00:14:33,207 --> 00:14:34,917
You put drugs in the water.

169
00:14:35,417 --> 00:14:37,585
Do you have any idea
what that's going to lead to when--

170
00:14:37,586 --> 00:14:40,546
It will no doubt be painful,
as it was 140 years ago.

171
00:14:40,547 --> 00:14:42,673
No, the forgetting won't be painful.

172
00:14:42,674 --> 00:14:44,510
The consequences are.

173
00:14:46,887 --> 00:14:49,431
Hundreds of people jumped off the stairs.

174
00:14:50,766 --> 00:14:54,936
Children starved to death
'cause their parents forgot to feed them.

175
00:14:54,937 --> 00:14:57,689
Okay, I know.
And this is where you come in.

176
00:14:58,232 --> 00:14:59,942
You and a person from each department.

177
00:15:03,529 --> 00:15:04,530
Wow.

178
00:15:05,405 --> 00:15:07,490
'Cause you need us to train everyone

179
00:15:07,491 --> 00:15:09,743
to function as a society again,
is that it?

180
00:15:12,788 --> 00:15:13,788
And then what?

181
00:15:13,789 --> 00:15:17,750
You and the other representatives
will need to forget as well.

182
00:15:17,751 --> 00:15:18,919
Right.

183
00:15:19,461 --> 00:15:20,921
But not... not you.

184
00:15:21,630 --> 00:15:24,590
Someone has to ensure
the transition is successful.

185
00:15:24,591 --> 00:15:26,676
Of course.
And why wouldn't that be you, Camille?

186
00:15:26,677 --> 00:15:27,803
Fuck you.

187
00:15:28,679 --> 00:15:31,431
I'll have my memories, yeah.

188
00:15:32,015 --> 00:15:33,392
But I'll be alone in that.

189
00:15:33,892 --> 00:15:35,978
- Oh.
- Each day-- Look at me.

190
00:15:36,562 --> 00:15:38,855
Each day until everything is reset,

191
00:15:38,856 --> 00:15:42,609
I'll have to remind my son who I am

192
00:15:44,319 --> 00:15:46,113
and hope he believes me.

193
00:15:46,905 --> 00:15:50,742
Or that there's enough of him left
to remember.

194
00:15:51,410 --> 00:15:52,828
[on radio] <i>Emerson for Camille Sims.</i>

195
00:15:55,664 --> 00:15:57,249
Yes, what... what is it?

196
00:15:58,041 --> 00:15:59,459
<i>A lot of people are heading up.</i>

197
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:04,172
Yeah, we're expecting that, Emerson.
Everyone wants to see Bernard Holland die.

198
00:16:04,173 --> 00:16:06,008
<i>No, I think it's something else.</i>

199
00:16:06,508 --> 00:16:09,969
<i>There's a couple hundred following the two
from Mechanical who led the rebellion.</i>

200
00:16:09,970 --> 00:16:13,056
<i>And another group not moving,
ten levels up on 55.</i>

201
00:16:15,142 --> 00:16:17,560
Do not let them get to water filtration.

202
00:16:17,561 --> 00:16:18,644
<i>Copy that.</i>

203
00:16:18,645 --> 00:16:21,398
[citizens clamoring]

204
00:16:26,778 --> 00:16:29,364
[chanting] Let us through! Let us through!

205
00:16:32,367 --> 00:16:34,786
Let us through!

206
00:16:36,663 --> 00:16:38,957
Hey! Hey!

207
00:16:43,253 --> 00:16:44,796
You know what's in the water.

208
00:16:45,506 --> 00:16:47,841
Step aside and none of your people
have to end up in Medical.

209
00:16:48,383 --> 00:16:49,384
Go home.

210
00:16:52,596 --> 00:16:54,263
You fuck this up

211
00:16:54,264 --> 00:16:59,019
and I will throw every single one
of your friends over the side.

212
00:17:02,606 --> 00:17:05,692
They wanna wipe our memories.
That goes for you too.

213
00:17:08,237 --> 00:17:09,694
Ready your weapons!

214
00:17:09,695 --> 00:17:11,489
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

215
00:17:11,490 --> 00:17:13,156
Hey, hey!

216
00:17:13,157 --> 00:17:15,910
Let's do this another day.
We've made our point.

217
00:17:15,911 --> 00:17:18,578
No! Let us through!

218
00:17:18,579 --> 00:17:19,914
[citizens chanting] Let us through!

219
00:17:19,915 --> 00:17:21,499
Let us through! Let us through.

220
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:23,710
[Juliette] Hey! Stop!

221
00:17:25,045 --> 00:17:27,256
Just put your weapons down. Now.

222
00:17:34,429 --> 00:17:36,390
Just... go home.

223
00:17:36,890 --> 00:17:38,684
They put drugs in the water.

224
00:17:39,268 --> 00:17:40,477
It's not true.

225
00:17:41,937 --> 00:17:43,438
It's vitamin D.

226
00:17:46,024 --> 00:17:47,192
Just go home.

227
00:17:49,570 --> 00:17:50,571
Go.

228
00:17:52,489 --> 00:17:55,576
You heard her. Let's go. Let's go.

229
00:18:03,584 --> 00:18:04,585
Well done.

230
00:18:06,420 --> 00:18:08,255
Your friends get to live another day.

231
00:18:12,718 --> 00:18:13,719
Watch her.

232
00:18:19,933 --> 00:18:21,727
[sighs] Good job holding the line.

233
00:18:22,269 --> 00:18:25,647
Out of precaution, I need you all
to stay here until after the cleaning.

234
00:18:26,148 --> 00:18:27,608
Wait.

235
00:18:29,568 --> 00:18:30,777
Is it true?

236
00:18:32,029 --> 00:18:33,196
About the water.

237
00:18:33,197 --> 00:18:34,906
Absolutely not.

238
00:18:34,907 --> 00:18:37,367
No, it's just another
Down Deep conspiracy theory.

239
00:18:46,585 --> 00:18:48,045
This is amazing.

240
00:18:48,587 --> 00:18:50,964
Feels like my stomach's in my ass.

241
00:18:51,507 --> 00:18:52,758
Wait. You never rode this?

242
00:18:53,425 --> 00:18:55,052
- No.
- [elevator rattles]

243
00:18:56,261 --> 00:18:58,179
[laughs]

244
00:18:58,180 --> 00:19:01,390
Yeah, and I was shit-faced
when I designed it.

245
00:19:01,391 --> 00:19:03,810
- [chuckles]
- [sighs] Yeah, fantastic.

246
00:19:07,397 --> 00:19:10,442
People obey you
even when they don't agree.

247
00:19:11,985 --> 00:19:13,779
That will be useful in the transition.

248
00:19:14,279 --> 00:19:16,155
Yeah, I'm not gonna help you.

249
00:19:16,156 --> 00:19:18,825
And even if I did,
you'd kill me anyway, so...

250
00:19:20,035 --> 00:19:21,494
- You're right.
- So that's your plan?

251
00:19:21,495 --> 00:19:23,746
You're just gonna get rid
of anyone who helps you?

252
00:19:23,747 --> 00:19:24,957
No, just you.

253
00:19:26,917 --> 00:19:28,836
You're still pissed off
I went into your home.

254
00:19:29,336 --> 00:19:30,753
I let you go, remember?

255
00:19:30,754 --> 00:19:32,130
No.

256
00:19:32,714 --> 00:19:35,300
I didn't remember, but I do now.

257
00:19:37,511 --> 00:19:39,596
Even if we take your memories again,

258
00:19:40,222 --> 00:19:43,975
I have no doubt you and I would still find
our way back to this exact same moment

259
00:19:43,976 --> 00:19:47,855
- because you don't ever quit.
- [inhales deeply]

260
00:19:48,564 --> 00:19:50,065
Check her left sleeve.

261
00:19:58,115 --> 00:20:00,284
So, yes, you'll die.

262
00:20:00,909 --> 00:20:02,911
Everyone else gets to live.

263
00:20:05,038 --> 00:20:07,499
I'll even make sure
you're remembered as a hero.

264
00:20:09,877 --> 00:20:10,919
Fuck you.

265
00:20:13,172 --> 00:20:14,798
Get her ready for the cleaning.

266
00:20:15,591 --> 00:20:17,217
Bring my husband to our apartment.

267
00:20:20,679 --> 00:20:21,972
[citizens chattering]

268
00:20:31,148 --> 00:20:32,274
[grunts]

269
00:20:34,443 --> 00:20:38,488
Shirl, this is...
I forget what he goes by now.

270
00:20:39,156 --> 00:20:40,949
Gone back to Gus.

271
00:20:42,492 --> 00:20:43,576
Who's this?

272
00:20:43,577 --> 00:20:46,245
Shirley. I've heard about you.

273
00:20:46,246 --> 00:20:47,414
Oh, yeah?

274
00:20:47,998 --> 00:20:52,961
Whatever you heard from him...
[chuckles] ...he's not wrong.

275
00:20:54,087 --> 00:20:55,756
Come on. Let's go.

276
00:21:00,135 --> 00:21:03,972
I know you're upset with me
for keeping Bernard alive.

277
00:21:07,559 --> 00:21:09,520
It wasn't an easy choice.

278
00:21:13,732 --> 00:21:17,653
Camille, I love you more than you know.

279
00:21:19,530 --> 00:21:21,323
But I also love our son.

280
00:21:25,577 --> 00:21:30,207
And you were asking me
to go along with taking his memories.

281
00:21:31,792 --> 00:21:33,544
Who will he be after that?

282
00:21:35,462 --> 00:21:36,839
Who would we be?

283
00:21:40,717 --> 00:21:42,219
I have no regrets.

284
00:21:45,973 --> 00:21:48,767
You talk about protecting our son,

285
00:21:50,602 --> 00:21:56,567
but you and Bernard
put every single person at risk.

286
00:22:02,698 --> 00:22:07,661
Before long all Anthony will know
about you is what I tell him.

287
00:22:12,708 --> 00:22:14,293
Bring him up in 15 minutes.

288
00:22:58,128 --> 00:23:01,339
I'm not taking a chance.
You'll go out without a suit.

289
00:23:01,340 --> 00:23:03,925
You can't do that.
You know that's against the Pact.

290
00:23:03,926 --> 00:23:07,971
It's actually not.
And a suit wouldn't change much anyway.

291
00:23:09,681 --> 00:23:11,099
[Chad] Ma'am, the sheriff is here.

292
00:23:20,651 --> 00:23:26,323
Did you actually say you wanna go out
or did some asshole pretend to hear it?

293
00:23:27,950 --> 00:23:29,283
[chuckles]

294
00:23:29,284 --> 00:23:32,204
- You've drawn a record-size crowd.
- [chuckles]

295
00:23:33,080 --> 00:23:35,249
- I said that to you, didn't I?
- Yeah.

296
00:23:35,832 --> 00:23:38,335
[inhales deeply] In my case,

297
00:23:39,378 --> 00:23:45,132
it's the perk of being hated by every man,
woman, child, and sanctioned pet.

298
00:23:45,133 --> 00:23:47,845
Why did you turn yourself in?

299
00:23:55,978 --> 00:24:01,066
[whispers] To give Walker
and your friends a distraction.

300
00:24:03,861 --> 00:24:05,361
No, no, no, no, no, no.

301
00:24:05,362 --> 00:24:09,658
Bernard. Camille forced me
to send Knox and Shirley away.

302
00:24:10,868 --> 00:24:13,202
She had guns pointed at them.

303
00:24:13,203 --> 00:24:16,956
She was gonna kill them.
I had no choice. It... It didn't work.

304
00:24:16,957 --> 00:24:18,374
But it's not over.

305
00:24:18,375 --> 00:24:20,710
You're being sent out to die.

306
00:24:20,711 --> 00:24:22,796
- Juliette.
- No. I'm still the mayor.

307
00:24:23,380 --> 00:24:24,922
Camille... she wants me to help her...

308
00:24:24,923 --> 00:24:27,049
- Stop--
- ...once the drugs set in, right?

309
00:24:27,050 --> 00:24:29,635
So she wants me to be there.
She wants me to make a deal with her.

310
00:24:29,636 --> 00:24:32,055
- And I said no. But maybe I can--
- Stop! Please.

311
00:24:34,474 --> 00:24:36,601
- You want to keep fighting?
- Yeah.

312
00:24:36,602 --> 00:24:38,769
Good. Because I still am.

313
00:24:38,770 --> 00:24:40,522
How is this fighting?

314
00:24:49,823 --> 00:24:53,577
[whispers] All eyes will be on me.

315
00:24:55,787 --> 00:25:01,793
Three months ago, your friends
pulled off a rebellion in your name.

316
00:25:02,961 --> 00:25:06,089
Since then I've seen who they are,

317
00:25:06,840 --> 00:25:08,175
what they can do.

318
00:25:09,176 --> 00:25:11,011
I was able to be a part of it.

319
00:25:12,262 --> 00:25:14,431
I've never had that in my life.

320
00:25:15,557 --> 00:25:18,268
The power that your people have.

321
00:25:19,061 --> 00:25:20,771
What they're capable of...

322
00:25:21,605 --> 00:25:25,150
given an opportunity, given a chance.

323
00:25:27,236 --> 00:25:30,697
My death is a chance.

324
00:25:32,199 --> 00:25:33,450
They won't waste it.

325
00:25:34,409 --> 00:25:35,660
Trust them.

326
00:25:35,661 --> 00:25:37,870
It's not about not trusting them.

327
00:25:37,871 --> 00:25:40,456
[breathes shakily]
But I could still find a way.

328
00:25:40,457 --> 00:25:42,083
What choice do we have?

329
00:25:42,084 --> 00:25:43,168
I don't--

330
00:25:46,463 --> 00:25:51,093
The most important thing
I can leave you with is this...

331
00:25:53,178 --> 00:25:54,972
that voice in the wall...

332
00:25:57,057 --> 00:25:59,560
I always thought it was a machine.

333
00:26:00,894 --> 00:26:03,272
An entity of some kind.

334
00:26:04,273 --> 00:26:06,024
A higher intelligence.

335
00:26:06,817 --> 00:26:08,110
It's not.

336
00:26:09,069 --> 00:26:12,573
Somewhere in Silo 1 is a person.

337
00:26:13,323 --> 00:26:14,616
Flesh and blood.

338
00:26:15,242 --> 00:26:18,035
No different or better than you or me.

339
00:26:18,036 --> 00:26:21,373
A person deciding our fates.

340
00:26:22,249 --> 00:26:23,375
[gasps]

341
00:26:24,376 --> 00:26:27,379
Turns out, after all this,

342
00:26:28,589 --> 00:26:33,218
the wizard is nothing more
than a man behind a curtain.

343
00:26:34,636 --> 00:26:37,472
And if that man
were in this room right now,

344
00:26:38,599 --> 00:26:41,143
I'd kill him with my bare hands.

345
00:26:41,727 --> 00:26:43,770
But I won't get that chance.

346
00:26:44,271 --> 00:26:46,857
But you can stop them, Juliette.

347
00:26:49,359 --> 00:26:53,447
You are the one their numbers missed.

348
00:26:54,781 --> 00:26:58,993
I know you don't want to be a leader,
so you don't see this.

349
00:26:58,994 --> 00:27:02,788
But you are more than a disrupter.

350
00:27:02,789 --> 00:27:04,666
You are a unifier.

351
00:27:06,502 --> 00:27:08,212
Your team proves that.

352
00:27:10,756 --> 00:27:12,174
They believe in you.

353
00:27:14,468 --> 00:27:18,388
And I believe in you. All of you.

354
00:27:20,057 --> 00:27:22,392
And there's one last thing on my mind.

355
00:27:24,102 --> 00:27:26,855
All the questions I won't see answered.

356
00:27:27,731 --> 00:27:32,110
They all come down to just one question.

357
00:27:33,111 --> 00:27:34,154
[door opens]

358
00:27:35,989 --> 00:27:36,990
Let's go.

359
00:27:39,952 --> 00:27:42,037
[Paul] <i>We do not know why we are here.</i>

360
00:27:43,956 --> 00:27:46,250
We do not know who built the Silo.

361
00:27:47,584 --> 00:27:52,422
We do not know why everything
outside the Silo is as it is.

362
00:27:53,006 --> 00:27:56,426
We do not know
when it will be safe to go outside.

363
00:27:58,345 --> 00:28:01,765
We only know that day is not this day.

364
00:28:17,656 --> 00:28:19,074
Do you have any last words?

365
00:28:19,950 --> 00:28:21,201
[inhales deeply]

366
00:30:07,850 --> 00:30:09,434
[citizens chattering]

367
00:30:46,889 --> 00:30:48,515
Why isn't he wearing a suit?

368
00:30:55,731 --> 00:30:57,733
[breathing heavily]

369
00:31:29,890 --> 00:31:33,894
[grunting]

370
00:31:47,199 --> 00:31:48,951
[sniffs, coughs]

371
00:32:05,425 --> 00:32:09,429
[panting]

372
00:32:39,960 --> 00:32:43,797
[panting]

373
00:32:56,393 --> 00:32:57,644
[exhales deeply]

374
00:33:30,385 --> 00:33:33,679
You know Bernard said that, um,

375
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:36,475
everything that we don't know
about the silos

376
00:33:37,100 --> 00:33:39,061
can be answered in one question.

377
00:33:41,021 --> 00:33:45,067
And I think it's as simple as, "Why?"

378
00:33:46,151 --> 00:33:48,611
We keep asking the same questions
over and over again.

379
00:33:48,612 --> 00:33:50,197
Why were the silos built?

380
00:33:51,949 --> 00:33:53,492
Why do people have to go into them?

381
00:33:54,618 --> 00:33:58,539
Why... Why couldn't the silos communicate
with each other, right?

382
00:33:59,665 --> 00:34:02,543
And if the Founders cared so fucking much,

383
00:34:03,752 --> 00:34:06,338
why would they put in a Safeguard
to be able to kill us?

384
00:34:09,091 --> 00:34:11,342
Maybe we aren't meant to know the answers.

385
00:34:12,469 --> 00:34:13,469
Why?

386
00:34:14,179 --> 00:34:16,723
- [rumbling]
- [clattering]

387
00:34:18,016 --> 00:34:19,809
[citizens shouting, exclaiming]

388
00:34:19,810 --> 00:34:22,311
Everybody... Everybody stay calm.

389
00:34:22,312 --> 00:34:24,022
We'll figure out what's going on.

390
00:34:24,648 --> 00:34:27,733
Hey! Stay calm. You're safer here.

391
00:34:27,734 --> 00:34:29,695
Take care of each other.
We're gonna figure it out.

392
00:34:30,320 --> 00:34:31,486
You planned this.

393
00:34:31,487 --> 00:34:34,449
No, I was in custody all day.

394
00:34:49,840 --> 00:34:51,048
[Ed] Fire in the hole.

395
00:34:51,049 --> 00:34:52,841
[explosion]

396
00:34:52,842 --> 00:34:55,429
- [loud rumbling]
- [citizens screaming]

397
00:34:56,471 --> 00:34:58,472
[Paul] Hey, hey. No, no. Don't leave.

398
00:34:58,473 --> 00:35:01,392
Don't leave. Hey, listen to me.

399
00:35:01,393 --> 00:35:02,644
Stay, stay.

400
00:35:03,478 --> 00:35:04,521
The mines.

401
00:35:05,189 --> 00:35:06,190
You...

402
00:35:07,399 --> 00:35:08,399
Fuck.

403
00:35:08,400 --> 00:35:09,734
[Paul] Stay calm! All right?

404
00:35:09,735 --> 00:35:11,904
Your friends may have just killed us all.

405
00:35:13,864 --> 00:35:15,824
Move! Out of the way!

406
00:35:17,201 --> 00:35:20,787
[heartbeat thumping on ultrasound]

407
00:35:23,248 --> 00:35:24,708
[doctor] There's that beautiful head.

408
00:35:27,085 --> 00:35:28,086
[Daniel] There she is.

409
00:35:28,712 --> 00:35:30,922
- [Helen chuckles]
- [doctor] Everything looks healthy.

410
00:35:30,923 --> 00:35:33,258
- You okay, there, chief?
- Yeah.

411
00:35:35,469 --> 00:35:39,097
[sighs] It's just feeling so real now.

412
00:35:40,098 --> 00:35:43,601
Now? Real... Did-Did you hear? Real now?

413
00:35:43,602 --> 00:35:45,770
[chuckles] You give him
the constant peeing,

414
00:35:45,771 --> 00:35:49,899
and the back pain
and leg cramps and swelling.

415
00:35:49,900 --> 00:35:51,776
If I could, half the world would love me

416
00:35:51,777 --> 00:35:53,653
- and the other half would hate me.
- [Helen] Right.

417
00:35:53,654 --> 00:35:56,948
- [Helen chuckles]
- Um... [chuckles] So can she fly?

418
00:35:56,949 --> 00:36:00,117
You're almost at the cutoff point,
but this weekend should be fine.

419
00:36:00,118 --> 00:36:01,703
Where are you off to?

420
00:36:02,663 --> 00:36:03,663
Atlanta.

421
00:36:03,664 --> 00:36:05,331
[chuckling] The Stensen thing?

422
00:36:05,332 --> 00:36:09,085
You won a golden ticket
to the chocolate factory? [chuckles]

423
00:36:09,086 --> 00:36:12,214
I had a few friends that applied for that
but couldn't get it.

424
00:36:12,798 --> 00:36:13,799
Do you know him?

425
00:36:15,259 --> 00:36:16,802
- We met him once.
- Hi?

426
00:36:17,386 --> 00:36:18,678
We met him. We did.

427
00:36:18,679 --> 00:36:20,805
Did you, uh, have to sign NDAs?

428
00:36:20,806 --> 00:36:23,141
I mean, he's famous
for making people sign NDAs.

429
00:36:40,033 --> 00:36:41,033
[Helen] Charlotte?

430
00:36:41,034 --> 00:36:42,910
- Hey.
- [Charlotte] Hey. Oh, my God.

431
00:36:42,911 --> 00:36:43,995
You're huge.

432
00:36:43,996 --> 00:36:45,079
- [laughs]
- [laughs]

433
00:36:45,080 --> 00:36:47,248
Is that not what I'm supposed to say?

434
00:36:47,249 --> 00:36:49,585
- Only if I wasn't huge. Hi.
- [chuckles] Hi.

435
00:36:50,586 --> 00:36:51,961
- [Charlotte] Oh. Hey.
- Hey.

436
00:36:51,962 --> 00:36:53,421
[Charlotte] Thanks for making the trip.

437
00:36:53,422 --> 00:36:56,674
- Ah! There you are. Oh, you look great.
- [Helen] Hey. [chuckles]

438
00:36:56,675 --> 00:37:00,011
- Thank you. Hi.
- Oh! Oh, it's so nice to see you.

439
00:37:00,012 --> 00:37:01,846
Hey. Hey.

440
00:37:01,847 --> 00:37:05,516
Well, I hope you guys like takeout.
Someone forgot to defrost the chicken.

441
00:37:05,517 --> 00:37:06,684
You evil fucker.

442
00:37:06,685 --> 00:37:09,187
- I did not say who that someone was.
- Okay.

443
00:37:09,188 --> 00:37:11,063
- For you.
- Ah, thank you. That's so kind.

444
00:37:11,064 --> 00:37:12,733
Come on in. Come on.

445
00:37:14,234 --> 00:37:15,443
[Anna laughs] <i>Oh.</i>

446
00:37:15,444 --> 00:37:18,197
Okay. How are the bridges in Portland?

447
00:37:18,989 --> 00:37:20,282
[Daniel] They're keeping me busy.

448
00:37:20,908 --> 00:37:22,617
We're looking to retrofit the Burnside,

449
00:37:22,618 --> 00:37:26,078
which is this-this beautiful
old Bascule bridge from 19--

450
00:37:26,079 --> 00:37:27,205
- [snores]
- Such an asshole.

451
00:37:27,206 --> 00:37:29,665
- Such a dick. Unbelievable. [laughs]
- [Anna] Char, stop.

452
00:37:29,666 --> 00:37:31,334
[laughing]

453
00:37:31,335 --> 00:37:34,879
And you? Driving a drone
for a trillionaire? How's that?

454
00:37:34,880 --> 00:37:36,381
It's a job.

455
00:37:39,927 --> 00:37:41,344
How is <i>The Oregonian</i>?

456
00:37:41,345 --> 00:37:45,139
[sighs] Understaffed, budget cuts,
dwindling subscriptions.

457
00:37:45,140 --> 00:37:46,641
The... The usual.

458
00:37:46,642 --> 00:37:48,643
And have you guys picked out a name yet?

459
00:37:48,644 --> 00:37:50,061
- [Helen gasps]
- [Anna] Aw...

460
00:37:50,062 --> 00:37:51,812
[Charlotte] We've been wondering. Come on.

461
00:37:51,813 --> 00:37:55,066
[sighs] Um... Daniel Keene II.

462
00:37:55,067 --> 00:37:56,692
- [groans] Shut up. Wow.
- Come on.

463
00:37:56,693 --> 00:37:58,903
- [Helen] Do you like it? [laughs]
- [Charlotte] Shut up.

464
00:37:58,904 --> 00:38:00,405
- [Anna] Oh, Jesus.
- [Charlotte] Wow.

465
00:38:01,573 --> 00:38:04,450
How about you two? Do you have any plans?

466
00:38:04,451 --> 00:38:05,536
Oh.

467
00:38:08,705 --> 00:38:11,332
Either way,
you both look really, really happy.

468
00:38:11,333 --> 00:38:13,417
- Yeah. We are.
- [Charlotte chuckles] Mmm.

469
00:38:13,418 --> 00:38:14,503
[Daniel] Yeah.

470
00:38:15,295 --> 00:38:16,922
I wish Mom and Dad could see you.

471
00:38:19,508 --> 00:38:20,716
If they were alive right now...

472
00:38:20,717 --> 00:38:22,718
Hmm.
They'd be clawing frantically to get out.

473
00:38:22,719 --> 00:38:23,970
...to get out of their coffins.

474
00:38:23,971 --> 00:38:26,264
- [groans] God.
- [groans] It's a sickness. It's so dark.

475
00:38:26,265 --> 00:38:29,643
- Yeah. [chuckles]
- But I'm still... I'm glad you came.

476
00:38:30,352 --> 00:38:31,978
We, uh, almost didn't.

477
00:38:31,979 --> 00:38:33,063
[Anna] Really?

478
00:38:33,647 --> 00:38:35,523
Daniel thinks your mom is still mad at us.

479
00:38:35,524 --> 00:38:39,026
- [Daniel] Oh, that is not... Uh, is she?
- Oh...

480
00:38:39,027 --> 00:38:40,695
You know, if she asks to speak with you,

481
00:38:40,696 --> 00:38:44,031
it usually means
she wants to bury the hatchet. Right?

482
00:38:44,032 --> 00:38:47,493
- [Daniel sighs]
- [Charlotte laughs] Sure. Yeah.

483
00:38:47,494 --> 00:38:48,912
[Anna laughs] We'll never know.

484
00:38:50,414 --> 00:38:55,042
How is everything?
Are... you guys ready for the big unveiling?

485
00:38:55,043 --> 00:38:58,338
Yeah, the light's
finally at the end of the tunnel.

486
00:38:58,922 --> 00:39:02,508
Except it's not daylight, it's a train!
[laughs]

487
00:39:02,509 --> 00:39:04,635
[chuckles]

488
00:39:04,636 --> 00:39:07,096
- Oh, wait. No, no, I'm sorry.
- No, no, it's okay. [chuckles]

489
00:39:07,097 --> 00:39:10,183
No, tomorrow's gonna be amazing.
Your mom is gonna be thrilled.

490
00:39:10,184 --> 00:39:14,479
And when it's all over,
we can finally go on a real honeymoon.

491
00:39:15,147 --> 00:39:18,066
You get one extra museum
'cause I was mean.

492
00:39:18,734 --> 00:39:20,235
Hmm. Could we go back like 30 seconds?

493
00:39:21,278 --> 00:39:22,362
Yes.

494
00:39:23,071 --> 00:39:24,280
- [Anna] Hmm.
- You said I'm sorry.

495
00:39:24,281 --> 00:39:26,115
Oh, very rare. [chuckles]

496
00:39:26,116 --> 00:39:27,992
- I've never heard that.
- I never hear any apology.

497
00:39:27,993 --> 00:39:30,036
- I mean, so rude.
- [Anna] She doesn't do it ever.

498
00:39:30,037 --> 00:39:31,370
- Rare?
- I haven't seen that before.

499
00:39:31,371 --> 00:39:33,331
- [Anna] It is.
- Right. Well, I'm not gonna apologize

500
00:39:33,332 --> 00:39:34,749
when I shove this down your throat.

501
00:39:34,750 --> 00:39:36,584
- I can do it. I'll do it.
- Take it. Take it.

502
00:39:36,585 --> 00:39:39,045
- [Helen] No.
- [Daniel] No problem.

503
00:39:39,046 --> 00:39:40,213
[Charlotte] They're hot.

504
00:39:40,214 --> 00:39:42,256
- [chuckles]
- [Charlotte] Great. [laughs]

505
00:39:42,257 --> 00:39:43,925
- [coughing]
- Ah. There it is.

506
00:39:43,926 --> 00:39:45,551
[water running]

507
00:39:45,552 --> 00:39:48,346
Oh, hey, hey, hey.
I'll do the rest. I'll do it.

508
00:39:48,347 --> 00:39:49,764
Keene family rule.

509
00:39:49,765 --> 00:39:52,350
Whoever makes dinner
gets the rest of the night off.

510
00:39:52,351 --> 00:39:54,268
I drove to get takeout. Just let me.

511
00:39:54,269 --> 00:39:56,312
- You're afraid I'm gonna do it wrong?
- [laughs]

512
00:39:56,313 --> 00:39:58,314
- Stepping away.
- Thank you.

513
00:39:58,315 --> 00:39:59,399
All right.

514
00:40:04,446 --> 00:40:06,490
I'm sorry we didn't make it back
when you were sick.

515
00:40:07,366 --> 00:40:09,492
You sent enough flowers
to fill three funeral homes, so...

516
00:40:09,493 --> 00:40:11,786
Oh, God. That's not what we...

517
00:40:11,787 --> 00:40:15,958
Look, Helen was newly pregnant
and dealing with her dad.

518
00:40:17,042 --> 00:40:20,670
Yeah. I could've come.
I don't... I don't know.

519
00:40:20,671 --> 00:40:22,047
Left her alone?

520
00:40:22,965 --> 00:40:27,051
Even Char doesn't think
you're that much of an asshole. [laughs]

521
00:40:27,052 --> 00:40:28,136
[Anna clears throat]

522
00:40:29,471 --> 00:40:30,888
Fought it before, fought it again.

523
00:40:30,889 --> 00:40:35,017
And I had Char with me, and my mother,

524
00:40:35,018 --> 00:40:37,770
and... and... and Henry. [chuckles]

525
00:40:37,771 --> 00:40:39,690
Oh, well, if Henry was there.

526
00:40:40,607 --> 00:40:42,901
I have known Henry
since I was 12 years old.

527
00:40:43,777 --> 00:40:45,946
He performs a very important service.

528
00:40:47,155 --> 00:40:50,909
He, um, comes in after my mother visits
and, like,

529
00:40:51,743 --> 00:40:54,287
sweeps up the emotional debris.

530
00:40:54,288 --> 00:40:55,581
[laughs]

531
00:40:56,832 --> 00:40:58,876
Anyway, I'm fine now.

532
00:41:00,002 --> 00:41:01,837
I'm grateful actually.

533
00:41:03,338 --> 00:41:07,092
For Charlotte, I mean, not the cancer.
Fuck cancer.

534
00:41:07,843 --> 00:41:09,261
Fuck cancer.

535
00:41:10,012 --> 00:41:11,470
Yeah. [chuckles]

536
00:41:11,471 --> 00:41:14,682
- [music on radio playing]
- [Helen, Charlotte laugh]

537
00:41:14,683 --> 00:41:18,769
Hmm. You know, the day we were married,
I didn't think I could love her more.

538
00:41:18,770 --> 00:41:20,439
And then this winter...

539
00:41:21,773 --> 00:41:23,775
Oof. [chuckles]

540
00:41:24,776 --> 00:41:28,989
And you know, it wasn't even
that she took care of me, which she did.

541
00:41:29,990 --> 00:41:31,366
It was that, um...

542
00:41:32,951 --> 00:41:33,952
[clicks tongue]

543
00:41:34,578 --> 00:41:36,872
So, when I was getting treatment,

544
00:41:37,664 --> 00:41:41,709
she would go to the floor below
and see the kids in the chairs.

545
00:41:41,710 --> 00:41:45,546
She's got some rough edges, your sister,
the Navy thing and all that.

546
00:41:45,547 --> 00:41:47,423
- Oh, really.
- [chuckles] She would, like...

547
00:41:47,424 --> 00:41:48,758
she would, like, try and rein it in,

548
00:41:48,759 --> 00:41:50,843
but she would just wind up swearing
like a sailor.

549
00:41:50,844 --> 00:41:52,929
"Fuck cancer
and fuck the horse it rode in on."

550
00:41:52,930 --> 00:41:56,098
And the kids just, like, loved it.

551
00:41:56,099 --> 00:41:57,850
[both laugh]

552
00:41:57,851 --> 00:42:01,063
And their parents loved it.
And then... [groans]

553
00:42:02,356 --> 00:42:04,358
Yeah, that's when I knew.
It was just, like...

554
00:42:05,484 --> 00:42:07,778
She is one tough lady, you know.

555
00:42:08,529 --> 00:42:12,199
But, man, she's kind. She's so kind.

556
00:42:19,164 --> 00:42:20,374
[breathes shakily]

557
00:42:21,667 --> 00:42:25,920
It's wild. We're actually letting people
into the silos tomorrow.

558
00:42:25,921 --> 00:42:28,464
And by people, you know,
I mean civilian swine, like you.

559
00:42:28,465 --> 00:42:31,426
Oh, thank you. Got it. [chuckles]

560
00:42:32,177 --> 00:42:34,345
I'm just glad
I finally get to talk about it.

561
00:42:34,346 --> 00:42:38,307
Yeah, I know. Those NDAs suck.
Sorry we couldn't tell you much.

562
00:42:38,308 --> 00:42:39,892
It's fine.

563
00:42:39,893 --> 00:42:44,564
Besides, signing those NDAs got us
the nicest home in West Slope.

564
00:42:44,565 --> 00:42:47,734
- Got us this Craftsman in Morningside.
- Hmm.

565
00:42:53,073 --> 00:42:56,117
It's been nuts... last six months.

566
00:42:56,118 --> 00:42:58,661
The second
Anna's cancer treatment was done,

567
00:42:58,662 --> 00:43:00,831
I mean,
she was back to all silos all the time.

568
00:43:02,958 --> 00:43:04,543
Have you heard of the Pact?

569
00:43:05,127 --> 00:43:06,669
- Mm-mmm.
- Oh, my God. Okay.

570
00:43:06,670 --> 00:43:11,132
So it's some crazy shit
she and Dr. C wrote.

571
00:43:11,133 --> 00:43:12,425
- Who?
- Oh, Victor.

572
00:43:12,426 --> 00:43:15,595
- Right.
- So, it is a book of rules

573
00:43:15,596 --> 00:43:20,558
about how to live underground
for 500 years without killing each other.

574
00:43:20,559 --> 00:43:23,561
- 500 years?
- I know. I said it was crazy.

575
00:43:23,562 --> 00:43:27,857
So is the fact that Stensen got
the silos operational in three years

576
00:43:27,858 --> 00:43:30,569
and, I mean, well,
people are living there right now.

577
00:43:31,486 --> 00:43:33,113
Not that I'm complaining.

578
00:43:34,198 --> 00:43:36,616
Anna and me, we get to work together

579
00:43:36,617 --> 00:43:38,368
and come back to this every night.

580
00:43:38,869 --> 00:43:41,830
Kind of reminds me
of where Dan and I grew up.

581
00:43:42,414 --> 00:43:43,582
You remember that?

582
00:43:44,333 --> 00:43:45,333
I think so.

583
00:43:45,334 --> 00:43:48,587
Sometimes it kind of feels
like someone else's story, but, um...

584
00:43:49,463 --> 00:43:50,546
Yeah, it's good enough.

585
00:43:50,547 --> 00:43:52,381
Your husband wants to go.

586
00:43:52,382 --> 00:43:54,008
Did he say it's past my bedtime?

587
00:43:54,009 --> 00:43:56,135
- He did. [laughs]
- Congressman.

588
00:43:56,136 --> 00:43:58,180
- [laughs]
- [grunts]

589
00:43:59,556 --> 00:44:00,557
[Charlotte] Mmm.

590
00:44:01,558 --> 00:44:03,059
Do you have time for breakfast tomorrow?

591
00:44:03,060 --> 00:44:04,268
Sure.

592
00:44:04,269 --> 00:44:07,648
I-I don't know.
We-We might have to go over early.

593
00:44:08,440 --> 00:44:11,067
Oh, I thought everything was figured out.

594
00:44:11,068 --> 00:44:14,195
If you get, um, pecan sticky buns
from Star Market,

595
00:44:14,196 --> 00:44:15,571
I'll do bacon and eggs.

596
00:44:15,572 --> 00:44:17,073
- Sounds like a plan.
- [Charlotte] Okay.

597
00:44:17,074 --> 00:44:18,909
- Good night.
- [Anna] Good night.

598
00:45:08,667 --> 00:45:09,668
What are you?

599
00:45:10,627 --> 00:45:11,919
Eighteen.

600
00:45:11,920 --> 00:45:13,421
- I'm one.
- [chime sounds]

601
00:45:13,422 --> 00:45:15,423
- [PA announcer] <i>If you need assistance...</i>
- One.

602
00:45:15,424 --> 00:45:17,049
<i>...see any representative
in a yellow jacket.</i>

603
00:45:17,050 --> 00:45:18,135
[Helen] This way.

604
00:45:19,136 --> 00:45:20,137
I'll walk ya.

605
00:45:30,314 --> 00:45:32,357
[representative] Daniel, Helen.

606
00:45:32,900 --> 00:45:33,984
- [Helen] Hey.
- [Daniel] Henry.

607
00:45:34,818 --> 00:45:36,903
- It's been a long time.
- [Henry] Congratulations.

608
00:45:36,904 --> 00:45:38,821
- [Helen chuckles]
- You both are glowing.

609
00:45:38,822 --> 00:45:41,157
Well, you are. I was just being inclusive.

610
00:45:41,158 --> 00:45:43,534
- [Daniel chuckles]
- Right. Thank you, Henry. [chuckles]

611
00:45:43,535 --> 00:45:45,119
We weren't sure you were gonna make it.

612
00:45:45,120 --> 00:45:47,622
No, I had to see how it all turned out.

613
00:45:47,623 --> 00:45:50,833
My advice is don't take the full tour.

614
00:45:50,834 --> 00:45:52,710
It's a lot of stairs and no elevators.

615
00:45:52,711 --> 00:45:54,212
I heard a rumor about that.

616
00:45:54,213 --> 00:45:56,631
Something about people
getting enough exercise.

617
00:45:56,632 --> 00:45:58,424
- Not my department.
- [Daniel chuckles]

618
00:45:58,425 --> 00:46:02,512
So, hey, can I borrow Daniel?
The senator wants a word with him.

619
00:46:02,513 --> 00:46:03,931
I'd love to see Charlotte.

620
00:46:04,598 --> 00:46:07,059
She's at Silo 1
with Anna and the senators.

621
00:46:07,559 --> 00:46:09,310
- Well, can Helen come?
- Well...

622
00:46:09,311 --> 00:46:11,103
Uh, it's a pretty long walk.

623
00:46:11,104 --> 00:46:12,813
- Yeah, no, uh...
- Yeah?

624
00:46:12,814 --> 00:46:13,898
...you go see Charlotte.

625
00:46:13,899 --> 00:46:15,858
I... [chuckles] I wanna take the tour.

626
00:46:15,859 --> 00:46:17,693
- [Henry] They're gonna feed you in there.
- Great.

627
00:46:17,694 --> 00:46:19,695
And when you're done,
come on over to Silo 1.

628
00:46:19,696 --> 00:46:22,532
- And the senator's gonna make a speech.
- [inhales sharply]

629
00:46:22,533 --> 00:46:27,036
[laughing] I know. I know, um,
she swears it's gonna be short.

630
00:46:27,037 --> 00:46:29,539
- There's music and fireworks later, so...
- Okay.

631
00:46:29,540 --> 00:46:31,249
I'm gonna talk to the senator
and come find you.

632
00:46:31,250 --> 00:46:32,334
- [Helen] Okay.
- Okay.

633
00:46:33,126 --> 00:46:34,586
Bye.

634
00:46:35,879 --> 00:46:37,880
[chimes sounds]

635
00:46:37,881 --> 00:46:40,175
[PA announcer]
<i>Silo tour will begin shortly.</i>

636
00:46:43,804 --> 00:46:45,847
[guide] If you were told
to come to Silo 18,

637
00:46:45,848 --> 00:46:47,390
uh, then you're in the right place.

638
00:46:47,391 --> 00:46:50,101
Uh, I'm Everett.
I'm one of your tour guides for today.

639
00:46:50,102 --> 00:46:52,103
- Oh, Samantha.
- Yes?

640
00:46:52,104 --> 00:46:54,480
Uh, you're assigned to 17,
which is just over there.

641
00:46:54,481 --> 00:46:56,190
- Oh. Right.
- Mm-hmm.

642
00:46:56,191 --> 00:46:59,068
Sorry. [chuckles]
How did you know my name?

643
00:46:59,069 --> 00:47:00,278
Augmented reality glasses.

644
00:47:00,279 --> 00:47:04,324
They, uh... They pull up all of your info
from the RFID in your badge. [chuckles]

645
00:47:05,242 --> 00:47:07,910
Uh, what if we're using
someone else's badge?

646
00:47:07,911 --> 00:47:10,831
Well, Helen, these also come
with facial recognition.

647
00:47:12,416 --> 00:47:15,126
Uh, Rachel will take the next 20 people.

648
00:47:15,127 --> 00:47:18,338
But, uh, if you're one of the ones
I greeted then follow me.

649
00:47:19,631 --> 00:47:21,507
[Rachel] All right. My group, over here.

650
00:47:21,508 --> 00:47:22,926
[Helen] Where are you from, Everett?

651
00:47:23,594 --> 00:47:26,346
- Oh, um, Hoddesdon.
- Hoddesdon?

652
00:47:26,889 --> 00:47:29,390
Well, um... it's a small town
just outside of London

653
00:47:29,391 --> 00:47:31,185
that even Londoners have never heard of.

654
00:47:32,644 --> 00:47:34,145
{\an8}[attendees chattering]

655
00:47:34,146 --> 00:47:35,981
{\an8}- [chime sounds]
- [camera shutter clicks]

656
00:47:36,982 --> 00:47:39,108
{\an8}[PA announcer] <i>A reminder to our guests:</i>

657
00:47:39,109 --> 00:47:40,359
{\an8}<i>Please ensure you visit</i>

658
00:47:40,360 --> 00:47:42,236
{\an8}- <i>the correct silo number...</i>
- [camera shutter clicks]

659
00:47:42,237 --> 00:47:45,114
{\an8}<i>...as shown on your identification badge.</i>

660
00:47:45,115 --> 00:47:46,200
{\an8}[camera shutter clicks]

661
00:47:47,534 --> 00:47:48,535
Daniel.

662
00:47:50,454 --> 00:47:52,914
- Victor. Hi. Hey. [chuckles]
- Daniel.

663
00:47:52,915 --> 00:47:54,124
- How are you?
- Hi.

664
00:47:54,958 --> 00:47:56,543
Victor, the senator wants to talk to him.

665
00:47:57,252 --> 00:47:59,296
- I'll get him there. Yeah.
- Okay. Thank you.

666
00:48:01,256 --> 00:48:02,715
Charlotte tells me you're gonna be a dad.

667
00:48:02,716 --> 00:48:04,592
Yeah, I'm terrified. [laughs]

668
00:48:04,593 --> 00:48:06,511
Congrats. No, you're gonna be great.

669
00:48:06,512 --> 00:48:08,138
- Thank you. I'll certainly try.
- Yeah.

670
00:48:12,226 --> 00:48:13,393
Pretty impressive, huh?

671
00:48:14,394 --> 00:48:15,603
It's more than I imagined.

672
00:48:15,604 --> 00:48:19,233
Yeah, trying to figure out
how we pulled it off?

673
00:48:21,985 --> 00:48:23,278
Maybe you're wondering...

674
00:48:24,071 --> 00:48:26,989
what it would've been like
if you said yes.

675
00:48:26,990 --> 00:48:28,200
I made the right choice.

676
00:48:28,867 --> 00:48:30,243
Of course you did.

677
00:48:30,244 --> 00:48:33,621
But it is
one of the biggest engineering projects

678
00:48:33,622 --> 00:48:34,872
in the history of the world.

679
00:48:34,873 --> 00:48:37,124
Nothing wrong with thinking
about the road not taken.

680
00:48:37,125 --> 00:48:38,417
[Daniel] Mm-hmm.

681
00:48:38,418 --> 00:48:39,503
We all do.

682
00:48:45,467 --> 00:48:48,595
You know, for what it's worth,
could've used you.

683
00:48:49,596 --> 00:48:51,056
You had plenty of great engineers.

684
00:48:51,765 --> 00:48:55,309
Sure. But I always felt
that you had a gift

685
00:48:55,310 --> 00:48:57,062
for seeing things other people missed.

686
00:48:59,147 --> 00:49:02,108
I gotta say, I was a little surprised

687
00:49:02,109 --> 00:49:03,651
when I heard
you were still on the project.

688
00:49:03,652 --> 00:49:05,486
I thought, you know,
a bunch of holes in the ground

689
00:49:05,487 --> 00:49:07,155
wouldn't exactly require your skills.

690
00:49:07,781 --> 00:49:09,490
You know, I-I thought the same thing.

691
00:49:09,491 --> 00:49:11,367
But the more I was here
working with Charlotte,

692
00:49:11,368 --> 00:49:15,080
the more I realized that, you know,
if these silos were ever to be used,

693
00:49:16,331 --> 00:49:18,040
it would
be after a massive traumatic event,

694
00:49:18,041 --> 00:49:19,667
and a lot of people would need help.

695
00:49:19,668 --> 00:49:23,171
I... The more you dive
into this whole project, the more you...

696
00:49:23,172 --> 00:49:27,134
the more you see how much there is to do
when it comes to managing mass trauma.

697
00:49:27,759 --> 00:49:29,011
Like the Pact.

698
00:49:29,553 --> 00:49:32,513
[chuckles] You heard about the Pact.

699
00:49:32,514 --> 00:49:35,516
[chuckles] Charlotte told you, right?

700
00:49:35,517 --> 00:49:36,642
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

701
00:49:36,643 --> 00:49:41,105
You and Anna writing a book about
how to live together for 500 years.

702
00:49:41,106 --> 00:49:43,734
No. To be honest, AI wrote it.
We just did some editing.

703
00:49:44,484 --> 00:49:48,363
And you know, hopefully, we'll never have
to find out if we did a good job.

704
00:49:50,324 --> 00:49:51,325
[Daniel scoffs]

705
00:49:56,455 --> 00:49:58,289
[Everett] Millions of people
from all around the world

706
00:49:58,290 --> 00:49:59,749
applied to join the project.

707
00:49:59,750 --> 00:50:02,293
And, uh, Dr. Stensen made it his goal
to have at least two people

708
00:50:02,294 --> 00:50:04,086
from every country on Earth.

709
00:50:04,087 --> 00:50:07,257
Two by two?
This really is Stensen's Ark, isn't it?

710
00:50:07,883 --> 00:50:08,926
Okay.

711
00:50:10,010 --> 00:50:14,138
This Silo was built to withstand
a 30 megaton nuclear explosion

712
00:50:14,139 --> 00:50:15,640
as close as two miles.

713
00:50:15,641 --> 00:50:18,518
Uh, these two big sliding doors each weigh
25 tons

714
00:50:18,519 --> 00:50:22,188
and can keep out particles
as small as 50 nanometers.

715
00:50:22,189 --> 00:50:23,856
Okay, Ms. Helen?

716
00:50:23,857 --> 00:50:25,983
How much did each Silo end up costing?

717
00:50:25,984 --> 00:50:28,569
Dr. Stensen plans
on releasing official figures soon.

718
00:50:28,570 --> 00:50:31,489
Are you one of the people who's
been living in here for the past year?

719
00:50:31,490 --> 00:50:34,450
Just under a year, but yes. Yes, I am.

720
00:50:34,451 --> 00:50:36,078
And you haven't gone insane?

721
00:50:37,621 --> 00:50:42,250
Okay, well, this is it, folks.
You are about to enter a Silo.

722
00:50:42,251 --> 00:50:44,168
- [clapping]
- [laughing] Yes.

723
00:50:44,169 --> 00:50:46,337
Uh, and I can guarantee you one thing...

724
00:50:46,338 --> 00:50:48,214
whatever it is
you think you are gonna see,

725
00:50:48,215 --> 00:50:49,423
you're not prepared.

726
00:50:49,424 --> 00:50:53,553
Um, so follow me,
uh, mind your step, and, um, yeah...

727
00:50:53,554 --> 00:50:56,598
Oh, Helen, at the risk of sounding
overly protective, would you take my arm?

728
00:50:57,933 --> 00:50:59,309
Okay. Thank you.

729
00:51:08,819 --> 00:51:09,819
It's this way.

730
00:51:09,820 --> 00:51:11,737
[person mumbling on mic]

731
00:51:11,738 --> 00:51:12,864
You gotta show your badge.

732
00:51:12,865 --> 00:51:15,157
- ["Here Comes the Flood" playing]
- [person] <i>One, two.</i>

733
00:51:15,158 --> 00:51:16,742
- Hey, fellas.
- [Daniel] Hi, how you doing?

734
00:51:16,743 --> 00:51:19,705
<i>Testing. Check. Let's go.</i>

735
00:51:28,881 --> 00:51:34,678
<i>♪ When the night shows
The signals grow on radios ♪</i>

736
00:51:36,513 --> 00:51:42,977
<i>♪ All the strange things
They come and go as early warnings ♪</i>

737
00:51:42,978 --> 00:51:47,357
<i>♪ Stranded starfish have
No place to hide ♪</i>

738
00:51:48,525 --> 00:51:53,989
<i>♪ Still waiting
For the swollen Easter tide ♪</i>

739
00:51:56,533 --> 00:51:59,786
<i>♪ There's no point in direction ♪</i>

740
00:52:00,871 --> 00:52:05,709
<i>♪ We cannot even choose a side ♪</i>

741
00:52:07,836 --> 00:52:10,589
- Is that Peter Gabriel? [chuckles]
- [chuckles] Yeah. Yeah.

742
00:52:11,548 --> 00:52:14,176
<i>♪ Here comes the flood ♪</i>

743
00:52:16,887 --> 00:52:21,725
<i>♪ We will say goodbye to flesh and blood ♪</i>

744
00:52:22,643 --> 00:52:23,644
How much did that cost?

745
00:52:24,478 --> 00:52:27,647
Nothing. He and his family wanted
to see inside a silo

746
00:52:27,648 --> 00:52:30,191
- just like everybody else in the world.
- Which silo?

747
00:52:30,192 --> 00:52:33,529
I think they're in 17. It's the best one.

748
00:52:35,239 --> 00:52:36,239
They're not all the same?

749
00:52:36,240 --> 00:52:39,325
[chuckles] No, they are.
[laughs] It's a Silo joke.

750
00:52:39,326 --> 00:52:41,787
- Never mind. Come on.
- Right. [chuckles]

751
00:52:43,330 --> 00:52:47,835
<i>♪ Dreamers, you're running dry ♪</i>

752
00:52:49,294 --> 00:52:50,920
That'll do. Thanks. [clears throat]

753
00:52:50,921 --> 00:52:52,923
[applause]

754
00:52:53,757 --> 00:52:55,007
Oh, but that was fabulous.

755
00:52:55,008 --> 00:52:57,343
- Thank you.
- Thank you, my friend. Oh, it was great.

756
00:52:57,344 --> 00:53:01,138
Now, would you like to see Silo 17
before your performance tonight?

757
00:53:01,139 --> 00:53:02,598
Daniel Keene,

758
00:53:02,599 --> 00:53:05,977
- you are a sight for these poor old eyes.
- [chuckles]

759
00:53:05,978 --> 00:53:08,271
Being out of Washington looks good on you.

760
00:53:08,272 --> 00:53:09,981
Oh. Well, you look good yourself, Senator.

761
00:53:09,982 --> 00:53:12,650
- Hmm. Still a politician.
- [chuckles]

762
00:53:12,651 --> 00:53:16,112
Now, tell me, why is it I'd hear from Anna
about you being in town,

763
00:53:16,113 --> 00:53:17,905
but you never stopped in for tea?

764
00:53:17,906 --> 00:53:19,949
I knew you were busy.
I didn't wanna interrupt.

765
00:53:19,950 --> 00:53:21,367
Well, you were right about that.

766
00:53:21,368 --> 00:53:24,412
It took a fuckton of work
to get this all up and running.

767
00:53:24,413 --> 00:53:26,123
- [Daniel] I bet.
- There he is.

768
00:53:27,541 --> 00:53:29,500
- I've got a bone to pick with you.
- Oh, yeah?

769
00:53:29,501 --> 00:53:31,294
Really could've used you on this, man.

770
00:53:31,295 --> 00:53:34,589
If we hadn't followed your suggestion
of 50 diggers,

771
00:53:34,590 --> 00:53:36,340
we'd have been five years behind schedule.

772
00:53:36,341 --> 00:53:38,134
- Mm-hmm.
- Could've used that brain of yours

773
00:53:38,135 --> 00:53:39,678
on all the other problems we had.

774
00:53:41,054 --> 00:53:43,222
[Thurman]
Per and I have to do a tech check.

775
00:53:43,223 --> 00:53:47,227
Hey, will you stick around?
I-I wanna discuss something with you.

776
00:53:48,061 --> 00:53:49,897
- Sure.
- [Thurman] Thank you.

777
00:53:53,483 --> 00:53:54,526
[Anna] Daniel.

778
00:53:55,402 --> 00:53:56,777
Hey.

779
00:53:56,778 --> 00:53:57,862
I'm sorry about breakfast.

780
00:53:57,863 --> 00:53:59,698
No, don't worry about it.
We'll do it tomorrow.

781
00:54:00,616 --> 00:54:02,408
- Yeah, that'd be great.
- Everything okay?

782
00:54:02,409 --> 00:54:05,494
Mm-hmm. Oh, yeah, yeah.
Just... they wanted us here early, so...

783
00:54:05,495 --> 00:54:06,788
[Daniel] Sure.

784
00:54:07,831 --> 00:54:09,332
Where's Charlotte?

785
00:54:09,333 --> 00:54:10,416
You know, I don't know.

786
00:54:10,417 --> 00:54:13,294
The last I saw, she was in the cafeteria
finishing off a mocha.

787
00:54:13,295 --> 00:54:15,379
So I guess she'll be out soon.

788
00:54:15,380 --> 00:54:19,718
[Thurman] Today, "my fellow Americans"
no longer seems adequate.

789
00:54:20,344 --> 00:54:23,012
This project is meant
to safeguard all of us

790
00:54:23,013 --> 00:54:26,349
regardless of color, creed or nationhood.

791
00:54:26,350 --> 00:54:30,978
So today,
I say my fellow people, all people...

792
00:54:30,979 --> 00:54:32,355
["Chopsticks" playing on keyboard]

793
00:54:32,356 --> 00:54:35,066
Per? Hey, Per. Per.

794
00:54:35,067 --> 00:54:37,027
You need to focus, okay?

795
00:54:38,737 --> 00:54:40,321
This project is the pinnacle

796
00:54:40,322 --> 00:54:44,368
of my long, and some might say
too long, career.

797
00:54:45,077 --> 00:54:46,911
Anna, Anna.

798
00:54:46,912 --> 00:54:49,080
Oh, um, not... not long enough.

799
00:54:49,081 --> 00:54:51,833
Yeah, honey, you leave me
hanging like that, I'm dead.

800
00:54:51,834 --> 00:54:53,709
- Okay?
- Won't happen again.

801
00:54:53,710 --> 00:54:57,088
But the reason I'm so proud...
Let's speed this up...

802
00:54:57,089 --> 00:55:01,175
The reason I'm so proud of this project is
a culmination of bipartisan cooperation,

803
00:55:01,176 --> 00:55:03,637
American can-do, yada yada yada... Okay.

804
00:55:04,471 --> 00:55:07,723
This is government
and industry working together

805
00:55:07,724 --> 00:55:10,977
on a level not seen since World War II.

806
00:55:10,978 --> 00:55:14,647
This applause will last,
I don't know, five, ten seconds.

807
00:55:14,648 --> 00:55:17,900
I'll start back up the third time
I raise my hand, okay?

808
00:55:17,901 --> 00:55:19,902
One, two, three... Now,

809
00:55:19,903 --> 00:55:21,571
I'm not gonna take all the credit.

810
00:55:21,572 --> 00:55:24,949
If we should thank anyone
for this magnificent undertaking,

811
00:55:24,950 --> 00:55:28,245
it's this man right here.

812
00:55:28,871 --> 00:55:32,498
A man whose genius
still exceeds his wealth.

813
00:55:32,499 --> 00:55:35,168
Per Stensen. Just... Come on.

814
00:55:35,169 --> 00:55:37,754
You don't want the applause dying
before you get here.

815
00:55:38,755 --> 00:55:42,758
Uh, I know some people think
this whole endeavor is a statement

816
00:55:42,759 --> 00:55:44,886
on how we've given up on the future,

817
00:55:44,887 --> 00:55:46,804
but that couldn't be farther
from the truth.

818
00:55:46,805 --> 00:55:49,892
This is a project of hope
and the human spirit.

819
00:55:52,186 --> 00:55:55,688
[Thurman] Now, I know Per's already
dreaming up the next big thing,

820
00:55:55,689 --> 00:56:01,903
building a spaceship to carry explorers
to the nearest inhabitable star system.

821
00:56:01,904 --> 00:56:04,405
Are you gonna have any dough left, Per?

822
00:56:04,406 --> 00:56:07,700
Now if that gets a laugh, just use it
to cover your cross back to the side.

823
00:56:07,701 --> 00:56:10,703
- [phone buzzing]
- If it doesn't, stay put, all right?

824
00:56:10,704 --> 00:56:15,666
Now, even if the silos are never needed,
and Lord hoping they won't be,

825
00:56:15,667 --> 00:56:20,588
they will be continually occupied
as long as the many threats remain.

826
00:56:20,589 --> 00:56:26,887
And they will be studied as prototypes
of sustainability and resource management.

827
00:56:28,514 --> 00:56:34,185
They will be studied as prototypes
of sustainability and resource management.

828
00:56:34,186 --> 00:56:35,687
Hey, what the fuck, Jeff?

829
00:56:36,563 --> 00:56:41,192
Come on. We gotta do better than ending
the most important speech of my life

830
00:56:41,193 --> 00:56:43,820
with the words "resource management,"
right?

831
00:56:44,696 --> 00:56:46,072
[Jeff] I cleared this with you yesterday.

832
00:56:46,073 --> 00:56:50,619
[Thurman] No.
This goes here. [speaking indistinctly]

833
00:56:51,745 --> 00:56:53,080
[Anna] You okay? What's going on?

834
00:56:57,835 --> 00:56:59,127
- Daniel?
- Huh?

835
00:57:02,381 --> 00:57:04,299
I'm just gonna... gonna take a second.

836
00:57:04,883 --> 00:57:05,884
Okay.

837
00:57:07,553 --> 00:57:08,554
[clears throat]

838
00:57:09,304 --> 00:57:10,346
[attendees chattering]

839
00:57:10,347 --> 00:57:12,391
[panting]

840
00:57:21,775 --> 00:57:23,568
[groans, pants]

841
00:57:23,569 --> 00:57:26,487
Hey, I'm getting a little, uh... uh...

842
00:57:26,488 --> 00:57:28,991
I don't know... worried.
Can you call me as soon as you can?

843
00:57:31,159 --> 00:57:32,160
[Henry] Daniel.

844
00:57:35,497 --> 00:57:37,583
Hey, uh, give me a call when you can.

845
00:57:40,502 --> 00:57:42,212
Daniel, you have to come back now.

846
00:57:44,298 --> 00:57:46,675
- Where's 18?
- I'll take you there later.

847
00:57:52,639 --> 00:57:53,640
[Daniel sighs]

848
00:58:01,106 --> 00:58:02,690
[Rachel] I can guarantee you one thing,

849
00:58:02,691 --> 00:58:06,737
no matter what it is you think
you're gonna see, you are not prepared.

850
00:58:07,404 --> 00:58:10,781
Uh, ma'am?
Ma'am, it's time to head in for dinner.

851
00:58:10,782 --> 00:58:12,868
Yeah, no. A hard pass.

852
00:58:17,873 --> 00:58:20,542
[panting]

853
00:58:29,134 --> 00:58:31,011
[Helen, distorted] <i>Hey, where are you?</i>

854
00:58:31,762 --> 00:58:33,805
Uh, Silo 1. Top of the berm.

855
00:58:34,640 --> 00:58:36,599
Yeah, can you get to that raised area
around your Silo?

856
00:58:36,600 --> 00:58:39,019
- The signal might be better.
- <i>Yeah, I'm almost there.</i>

857
00:58:39,686 --> 00:58:43,440
[pants] The Silo is so fucking weird.

858
00:58:44,066 --> 00:58:45,358
It's so deep you can't see the bottom

859
00:58:45,359 --> 00:58:48,237
and there really aren't
any fucking elevators.

860
00:58:49,238 --> 00:58:50,280
- <i>Where are you?</i>
- Hel...

861
00:58:50,948 --> 00:58:51,948
I can't--

862
00:58:51,949 --> 00:58:54,283
I'm coming to 1, so just stay there.

863
00:58:54,284 --> 00:58:56,869
No, no, no, no. No. I'm-I'm coming to 18.

864
00:58:56,870 --> 00:58:59,957
You're at 18? I can't... I can't see you.

865
00:59:01,625 --> 00:59:03,626
I'm coming to 18. Just stay there.

866
00:59:03,627 --> 00:59:05,795
Okay, okay. I can hear you.
I can hear you.

867
00:59:05,796 --> 00:59:07,255
<i>I got a text from Charlotte.</i>

868
00:59:07,256 --> 00:59:09,048
But I don't know.
I don't think she wrote it.

869
00:59:09,049 --> 00:59:10,175
Who wrote it then?

870
00:59:10,926 --> 00:59:11,927
<i>I-I don't know.</i>

871
00:59:12,845 --> 00:59:14,847
[Helen] I think I see you. Wave.

872
00:59:15,931 --> 00:59:18,350
To your right. Look for the big red dot.

873
00:59:19,434 --> 00:59:20,769
[sighs] Hey.

874
00:59:21,812 --> 00:59:23,730
Hi, Congressman. [chuckles]

875
00:59:24,731 --> 00:59:27,775
[explosion]

876
00:59:27,776 --> 00:59:31,320
- [ringing]
- [Daniel, distorted gasps]

877
00:59:31,321 --> 00:59:33,574
[panting]

878
00:59:35,450 --> 00:59:36,743
[grunts]

879
00:59:38,078 --> 00:59:40,998
[pants]

880
00:59:41,832 --> 00:59:45,794
[pants]

881
00:59:59,766 --> 01:00:00,976
Daniel. [gasps]

882
01:00:03,228 --> 01:00:06,022
Helen. Helen!

883
01:00:06,023 --> 01:00:07,356
- No. No!
- There's no point.

884
01:00:07,357 --> 01:00:09,234
[Daniel grunting] No!

885
01:00:12,821 --> 01:00:15,865
[The Algorithm] <i>Seek shelter in
the nearest Silo as quickly as possible.</i>

886
01:00:15,866 --> 01:00:20,119
- [grunting] Helen! Helen!
- [Henry] Daniel!

887
01:00:20,120 --> 01:00:22,330
Helen! No!

888
01:00:22,331 --> 01:00:23,790
We're trying to save your life.

889
01:00:28,795 --> 01:00:31,131
- Fuck! Run!
- [grunts]

